Subject: Editorial critical of proposed obscenity law (re: cards, games)
Date: 22 Dec 1994 20:39:34 -0000
Message-ID: <3dco66$da9@sunforest.mantis.co.uk>


The Ottawa Citizen (19dec94, A8) ran an editorial that was highly
critical of a recent Canadian Justice Committee's recommendations
to broaden the legal definition of obscenity.  The Committee was
supposed to consider narrow legislation that would ban certain
"serial killer cards and board games", but after deciding these
were just the tip of an evil iceberg, they called for a new and broader
legal definition of "obscenity" that would prohibit various
computer games and bulletin boards depicting "horror, cruelty, or violence.

In his editorial, Peter Calamai writes that the proposal of
an "even more sweeping [obscenity] law ... would make criminals
out of half the teenagers who use computers in this country".
Later he writes, "Justice Minister Allan Rock should reject
the committee's sweeping censorship proposals."

Allan Rock is required to respond to the committee's report, in writing,
within 150 days (by mid-April, '95).



